---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS } from '../lib/data';
---

<Layout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How Kerala Web Watch audits government websites: what it checks, and its limits."
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>

  <p>
    <strong>Current status:</strong> every site's light check (reachability, TLS, response time,
    security headers) already runs every 6 hours. The deep audit &mdash; accessibility,
    performance, content and GIGW-compliance checks, plus a screenshot &mdash; is being rolled out
    site by site; until a site's first deep audit completes, it shows "Not yet audited" below the
    light-check status. Nothing on this site is invented or estimated in the meantime.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Passive only.</strong> We fetch pages like a browser would &mdash; the homepage and
      a bounded set of same-site links. No path guessing, no port scans, no form submissions, no
      login attempts. Security findings come only from response headers and page contents.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Polite.</strong> At most one request per second per site, bounded page/PDF counts
      per audit, and a `robots.txt`-respecting, self-identifying User-Agent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reproducible.</strong> Every check is a pure function of the fetched response to a
      pass/warn/fail/not-applicable result with evidence attached.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Explainable.</strong> Every finding will show what it means for a citizen and how a
      webmaster can fix it &mdash; never a bare internal check id.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Charitable.</strong> A site is only called "down" after two consecutive failed
      checks. A site unreachable only from our vantage point is "unverifiable", not "down".
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Two tiers of checks</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>light check</strong> (planned every 6 hours, for every site) will look at
    reachability, HTTP status, redirects, TLS certificate validity, response time and security
    headers. A <strong>deep audit</strong> (planned daily, on a rolling batch) will add
    accessibility, performance, content and GIGW-compliance checks, plus a screenshot.
  </p>

  <h2>Status definitions</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Down</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS.down}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Possibly hijacked</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS.hijacked}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Broken</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS.broken}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unverifiable</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS.unverifiable}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Healthy</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS.healthy}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Needs work</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS['needs-work']}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Poor</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS.poor}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Not yet audited</td><td>{STATUS_DESCRIPTIONS.unaudited}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A status word is never shown alone on this site (<a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/blob/main/docs/DECISIONS.md">ADR-026</a>):
    every "Down" or "Broken" site page states the specific reason &mdash; a DNS failure, a refused
    connection, an invalid certificate, a blank page, and so on &mdash; not just the badge above.
  </p>

  <p>
    See <a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/blob/main/docs/DESIGN.md">docs/DESIGN.md</a>
    in the repository for the full design, including the complete check catalogue once it exists.
  </p>
</Layout>
